  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nDeep layer moisture and humidity will increase Thu into Fri, as\na southwest flow develops around the periphery of a Bermuda \nhigh. Dewpoints still expected to rise into the 70s, with PWAT \nanomalies increasing to +1 to +2 STDEV. Convection will be most \nlikely during the diurnally favored afternoon to early evening \nhours. There looks to be a lack of synoptic scale forcing much \nof the time, as a frontal system is expected to be positioned \nnorth/west of Lake Ontario with no significant push to the south\ndue to the strength of the Bermuda high. So have lowered NBM \nPoPs to cap values at 60-70% both days. Thu/Fri will likely not \nbe washouts, although scattered to numerous showers/T-storm \nshould develop in the afternoon. CAPE/shear magnitudes combined \nwith subtle forcing look somewhat modest for widespread severe \nstorms, although a strong/severe storm or two cannot be ruled \nout. AI NWP convective hazards forecast continues to show some \npotential for severe storms Thu/Fri. Also with a humid/moist \nenvironment and wet antecedent ground conditions in place, there\nis the potential for isolated flash flooding where persistent \nor repeated downpours occur. There is a Marginal Risk (level 1 \nof 4) of excessive rainfall both days.\n\nThe front may finally start to settle south/east across the \narea over the weekend although it may wash out by Sun. So there \nlooks to be no discernible change in the moist/humid air mass, \nwith the better likelihood of showers/T-storms on Sat compared \nto Sun.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nWith our area positioned along the northwest periphery of the \nBermuda high, increasing heat and humidity are expected Thu into\nFri. Although high temperatures will likely remain below 90F, \ndewpoints rising into the lower/mid 70s will still result in \nheat indices(feels-like temperatures) in the mid to perhaps \nupper 90s in valley areas south of Albany. Heat Advisories may \neventually be needed for parts of the mid Hudson Valley if \nconfidence in > 95F heat indices increases.",
